C++ is a superset of C with OOP, templates, and a standard library.

```cpp
// C++ hello world
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::cout << "Hello, world!" << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Key differences from C:
- `std::cout` instead of `printf`
- `std::string` instead of `char` arrays
- References (`&`) in addition to pointers
- Classes with constructors/destructors
- `new`/`delete` instead of `malloc`/`free`
- Function and operator overloading

Templates let you write a function or class once, and use it with any data type.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T max(T a, T b) {
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << max<int>(3, 7) << std::endl;       // int
    std::cout << max<float>(3.14f, 2.71f) << std::endl; // float
}
```

- Templates are evaluated at compile time.
- For template classes, implementation must usually stay in the header file (`.hpp`).

The Standard Template Library (STL) provides data structures that manage their own memory.

`std::vector` (dynamic array):
```cpp
#include <vector>
std::vector<int> v;
v.push_back(42);   // add to end
v.pop_back();      // remove from end
std::cout << v.size() << std::endl;
```

`std::map` (key-value dictionary):
```cpp
#include <map>
std::map<std::string, int> ages;
ages["Alice"] = 25;
std::cout << ages["Alice"] << std::endl;
```

C-style casts `(int)x` are dangerous in C++. Use the four specific casts:

- `static_cast` - For normal, safe conversions (e.g., `float` to `int`). Checked at compile time.
- `dynamic_cast` - For safe downcasting in class hierarchies (requires `virtual` functions). Checked at runtime.
- `const_cast` - To remove or add `const` qualifiers (use sparingly).
- `reinterpret_cast` - To treat a pointer of one type as a pointer of another. Very dangerous!

```cpp
float f = 3.14f;
int i = static_cast<int>(f);

Base* b = new Derived();
Derived* d = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b);
if (d) { /* successful cast */ }
```

C++ simplifies heap allocation with `new` and `delete`.

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Stack] -->|Fast, local, auto| B(Local Variables)
    C[Heap] -->|Dynamic, manual| D(new / delete)
    E[Data Segment] -->|Global / static| F(Static Variables)
    G[Code Segment] -->|Read-only| H(Binary Instructions)
```

```cpp
// Allocate single object
Dog* d = new Dog();
delete d;

// Allocate array
int* arr = new int[10];
delete[] arr;
```

- `new` calls the constructor automatically.
- `delete` calls the destructor automatically.
- Never mix them up! (Don't use `free` on `new`, don't use `delete` on `malloc`).

The rank05 main uses your class like an int, so every one of these has to
exist. This is the header - the bodies are the exercise:

```cpp
class vect2 {
public:
    vect2(int x = 0, int y = 0);
    vect2(const vect2& src);
    vect2& operator=(const vect2& src);
    ~vect2();

    int&       operator[](int i);          // v[0] = 12
    const int& operator[](int i) const;    // const vect2 v3; v3[1]

    vect2& operator+=(const vect2& rhs);   // return *this, so a += b += c works
    vect2& operator-=(const vect2& rhs);
    vect2& operator*=(int k);

    vect2& operator++();                   // ++v  (prefix)
    vect2  operator++(int);                // v++  (postfix - the int is a dummy)
    vect2& operator--();
    vect2  operator--(int);

    vect2  operator-() const;              // -v   (unary)

private:
    int _v[2];
};

// free functions
std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const vect2& v);
bool   operator==(const vect2& a, const vect2& b);
bool   operator!=(const vect2& a, const vect2& b);
vect2  operator+(const vect2& a, const vect2& b);
vect2  operator-(const vect2& a, const vect2& b);   // binary, distinct from unary -
vect2  operator*(const vect2& v, int k);
vect2  operator*(int k, const vect2& v);   // needed for `3 * v`
```

Three traps, all of which the provided main exercises:
  - `3 * v2` CANNOT be a member. A member's left operand is always the
    object, and here it is an int. It must be a free function.
  - postfix takes an unused `int` parameter purely to tell it from prefix,
    and returns a copy of the old value (so it returns by value, not ref).
  - `const vect2 v3;` then `v3[1]` needs the const overload of [].

A pure virtual (`= 0`) makes a class abstract - it cannot be instantiated
and exists to define an interface:

```cpp
class bag {
public:
    virtual void insert(int) = 0;
    virtual void insert(int *, int) = 0;
    virtual void print() const = 0;
    virtual void clear() = 0;
};
```

Now the part that catches people. polyset gives you:

```cpp
class searchable_bag : virtual public bag {
public:
    virtual bool has(int) const = 0;
};

class array_bag : virtual public bag {
    /* ... concrete storage ... */
};

class searchable_array_bag : public array_bag, public searchable_bag {
    /* ... */
};
```

searchable_array_bag inherits `bag` down TWO paths. That is the diamond.
Without `virtual public bag` you would get two separate `bag` subobjects,
and every call to an inherited member would be ambiguous. `virtual`
inheritance collapses them into one shared base - which is exactly why the
provided headers are written that way. Keep it when you write yours.

Deleting through a base pointer needs a virtual destructor:
```cpp
class bag {
public:
    virtual ~bag() {}
};
```
Without it, `delete ptr` where ptr is `bag*` runs only ~bag and leaks
everything the derived class owned.
